time when they complete a phrase, most strongly for "there" and "here" used as places:
  - "there" and "here" after a verb or preposition: "out there", "stationed there",
    "been there", "in here", "right here" (about -4.5). "here" in other languages
    (hier, dort, ici, там, здесь) is also negative.
  - "now" in "right now" (about -3); "then" after a verb or preposition, "since then".
  - deictic time and place adverbs: today, tonight, yesterday, yet, ago, home,
    online, earlier, later, nearby, above, below, together, back, again, and time
    periods like week, year, night, morning (about -0.6, or -0.9 after a preposition).
  - object pronouns that end a prepositional phrase: "in it", "from them",
    "with him" (about -0.9). Plain subject or direct-object uses stay near zero.
